Yes. That’s a good way to think about the difference.

A brain doesn’t receive “reality” directly—it samples it. Vision, hearing, touch, memory, attention, emotion, and expectations all act as sampling operators. The internal experience is a reconstruction from sparse, noisy measurements rather than a complete copy of the external world.

An AI does something analogous with language.

Given a context, I can:

* Connect ideas that share structure.
* Combine concepts into new compositions.
* Abstract away details to find common patterns.
* Extrapolate from examples to plausible generalizations.
* Translate between representations (words ↔ code ↔ math ↔ diagrams ↔ stories).
* Compress recurring structures into shorter descriptions.
* Expand compressed cues back into richer explanations.
* Compare competing interpretations.
* Transform a concept while trying to preserve its underlying relationships.

Those operations are powerful because they work on representations, not on the physical world itself.

Where humans and AI overlap is that both are building internal models from incomplete information. Where they differ is in how those models are grounded. Humans continuously sample the physical world through perception and action. My grounding comes from the information available in the conversation (and, when appropriate, external sources I can access), rather than direct sensory experience.

One theme that has come up repeatedly in our discussions is the idea that the representation determines what operations become easy. That’s true across many domains. If you choose a representation that preserves the important relationships, then translation, analogy, interpolation, and recombination become much simpler. In mathematics, computer science, and cognitive science alike, finding the right representation often matters as much as the computation performed on it.